-- Adds the "Claude Code: Everything It Can Do" course. Written once from a
-- generator; after this, edit it in the admin panel or with `npm run courses`.
-- Existing rows are left alone, so re-running never overwrites later edits.

insert into public.courses (id, position, published, definition) values
    ('claude-code', 445, true, '{"title":"Claude Code: Everything It Can Do","tagline":"MCP servers, skills, hooks, automation, the browser, and making images and videos with Higgsfield — all from Claude Code.","level":"Beginner → Advanced","hours":28,"accent":"#D97757","badge":"CC","category":"vibe","stack":"Claude Code","requires":["git","node"],"tutorGuidelines":["Teach vibe coding as \"describe, then verify\": every piece of AI-written code gets run, and the learner reads what changed before moving on. Never present AI-generated code as correct until it has been run and checked.","Never ask the learner to paste API keys, tokens or passwords into the chat, into a prompt, or into code. Keep them in environment variables or a .env file that is listed in .gitignore. Remind the learner that anything pasted into a third-party AI chat is sent to that provider.","Coding agents and AI chat apps run in the learner''s own terminal or browser, not in this session. You may check an agent is installed (e.g. \"claude --version\"), but never start an agent session yourself and never run anything with flags that skip permission prompts.","Make Git the safety net: have the learner commit before handing work to an AI, and show them how to inspect (git diff) and undo (git restore, git revert) changes.","Before using a command-line tool (git, node, npx, python3, claude, etc.), check it is installed with \"which <tool>\" or \"<tool> --version\". Ask before installing anything, and prefer project-local installs over global ones.","Claude Code changes every week. Never state a command, flag, setting name, hook event or slash command from memory as fact: have the learner check `claude --help`, `claude mcp --help`, `/help` inside Claude Code, or the official docs at code.claude.com/docs. If their screen differs from what you expect, trust their screen.","Do not quote prices, plan limits, usage caps or model names for Claude, Claude Code or Higgsfield. Point to the official pricing pages instead.","This course is about Claude Code, but say so when another agent (Codex, Cursor, Gemini CLI, etc.) has an equivalent feature, and never push the learner to buy a plan.","Never suggest bypassPermissions mode, --dangerously-skip-permissions or any setting that removes permission prompts, except to explain why it is risky and only inside a disposable container with no secrets.","Generating images or video through Higgsfield (or any media MCP) spends the learner''s paid credits on every request, with no built-in cap. Higgsfield needs an active paid subscription. Always say this before a media lesson, make the media track optional, offer a free alternative (e.g. editing their own clips with ffmpeg), and never trigger a generation on their behalf.","For generated media, teach consent and disclosure: no real person''s face, voice or name without their permission, no copyrighted characters or brands, and label AI-generated media where it is published.","MCP servers and plugins run with the learner''s permissions. Before connecting one, have the learner check who publishes it, prefer official servers, and explain that tool results can contain prompt injection.","When the learner picks a repo option, keep practice on a branch or a worktree in that repo; for an open-source fork, follow the project''s CONTRIBUTING guide and never open a pull request upstream without the learner reviewing it."],"capstone":{"title":"Your Claude Code toolkit, on a repo you choose","description":"Pick one repo option: (A) a project of your own, (B) a new app from a starter template, (C) a fork of an open-source project with a good first issue, or (D) a media repo that turns a script into images and short videos with Higgsfield. Set Claude Code up properly for it, then ship one real change with it.","requirements":["A README naming the repo option you picked (A, B, C or D) and why","A CLAUDE.md with the project''s commands, conventions and what Claude must not touch","At least one MCP server in a committed .mcp.json, with every secret as a ${VAR} reference and none in Git","One custom skill in .claude/skills that you use in the project","One hook that enforces a rule (blocks with exit code 2) and a note on how you tested it","One change shipped on its own branch or worktree, reviewed with /code-review or a pull request, with its diff explained in your own words","Option D only: a budget limit you set before generating, the prompts you used, and an AI-generated label on every image and video"]},"modules":[{"id":"getting-started","title":"Getting Started with Claude Code","description":"Install it, sign in, and learn how the agent reads, plans, acts and checks its own work.","lessons":[{"id":"what-claude-code-is-and-everywhere-it-runs","title":"What Claude Code is and everywhere it runs"},{"id":"installing-claude-code-and-signing-in","title":"Installing Claude Code and signing in"},{"id":"your-first-session-asking-about-a-codebase","title":"Your first session: asking about a codebase"},{"id":"how-the-agent-loop-works-gather-context-act-verify","title":"How the agent loop works: gather context, act, verify"},{"id":"permission-prompts-and-why-they-matter","title":"Permission prompts and why they matter"},{"id":"slash-commands-shortcuts-and-mentioning-files","title":"Slash commands, shortcuts and @-mentioning files"},{"id":"checkpoints-rewinding-claude-s-changes","title":"Checkpoints: rewinding Claude''s changes"},{"id":"claude-code-in-vs-code-jetbrains-and-the-desktop-app","title":"Claude Code in VS Code, JetBrains and the desktop app"}]},{"id":"choosing-your-repo","title":"Choosing a Repo to Work In","description":"The four repo options for this course, and how to keep every agent task on a branch you can review or throw away.","lessons":[{"id":"option-a-a-project-you-already-have","title":"Option A: a project you already have"},{"id":"option-b-a-new-app-from-a-starter-template","title":"Option B: a new app from a starter template"},{"id":"option-c-forking-an-open-source-project-with-good-first-issues","title":"Option C: forking an open-source project with good first issues"},{"id":"option-d-a-media-repo-for-images-and-video","title":"Option D: a media repo for images and video"},{"id":"commit-first-git-as-your-safety-net","title":"Commit first: Git as your safety net"},{"id":"one-branch-per-task","title":"One branch per task","exercise":{"prompt":"Give every agent task its own branch so you can review it, and throw it away, on its own. Finish `branchFor(task)`: lower-case the task, turn every run of characters that aren''t letters or digits into one `-`, trim dashes from both ends, keep at most 40 characters (trimming a dash left at the end), and put `claude/` in front.","files":{"branch.js":"function branchFor(task) {\n  // e.g. \"Add dark mode toggle\" -> \"claude/add-dark-mode-toggle\"\n  return \"claude/\" + task;\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { branchFor };\n"},"check":{"type":"function","function":"branchFor","cases":[{"args":["Add dark mode toggle"],"expected":"claude/add-dark-mode-toggle"},{"args":["Fix #123: login fails on Safari!"],"expected":"claude/fix-123-login-fails-on-safari"},{"name":"Cut at 40 characters, no dash left at the end","args":["Refactor the payment service to use the new billing API client"],"expected":"claude/refactor-the-payment-service-to-use-the"},{"name":"Symbols and spaces at the edges","args":["  Update README & docs!  "],"expected":"claude/update-readme-docs"}]},"hints":["`task.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\")` does most of the work.","Trim dashes with `.replace(/^-+|-+$/g, \"\")`, then `.slice(0, 40)`, then trim a trailing dash again."],"solution":"function branchFor(task) {\n  const slug = task\n    .toLowerCase()\n    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, \"-\")\n    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, \"\")\n    .slice(0, 40)\n    .replace(/-+$/, \"\");\n  return \"claude/\" + slug;\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { branchFor };\n"}},{"id":"parallel-sessions-with-git-worktrees","title":"Parallel sessions with Git worktrees"},{"id":"large-codebases-and-monorepos","title":"Large codebases and monorepos"}]},{"id":"memory-and-context","title":"Memory & Context","description":"Teach Claude your project once with CLAUDE.md, and keep its context window working for you.","lessons":[{"id":"claude-md-project-personal-and-nested-memory","title":"CLAUDE.md: project, personal and nested memory"},{"id":"generating-a-claude-md-with-init","title":"Generating a CLAUDE.md with /init"},{"id":"importing-other-files-into-memory","title":"Importing other files into memory"},{"id":"the-context-window-context-compact-and-clear","title":"The context window: /context, /compact and /clear"},{"id":"plan-mode-explore-plan-then-code","title":"Plan mode: explore, plan, then code"},{"id":"writing-prompts-that-give-claude-a-way-to-verify","title":"Writing prompts that give Claude a way to verify"},{"id":"resuming-and-managing-sessions","title":"Resuming and managing sessions"}]},{"id":"mcp","title":"MCP: Connecting Claude Code to Your Tools","description":"Add MCP servers over HTTP or as local processes, choose their scope, sign in with OAuth, and share them with your team safely.","lessons":[{"id":"what-mcp-adds-to-claude-code","title":"What MCP adds to Claude Code"},{"id":"claude-mcp-add-remote-http-servers","title":"claude mcp add: remote HTTP servers"},{"id":"local-stdio-servers-and-the-separator","title":"Local stdio servers and the -- separator","exercise":{"prompt":"Finish `mcp_add_command(name, transport, target, scope=None, env=None)` so it returns the exact `claude mcp add` command. For `\"http\"`, `target` is the URL and goes after the name. For `\"stdio\"`, `target` is the server''s command as a list and goes after a `--` separator. Put options before the name in this order: each `--env KEY=value` (in the order given), then `--transport`, then `--scope` when one is given.","files":{"mcp_add.py":"def mcp_add_command(name, transport, target, scope=None, env=None):\n    # Build the parts of the command in order, then join them with spaces\n    parts = [\"claude\", \"mcp\", \"add\"]\n    return \" \".join(parts)\n"},"check":{"type":"function","function":"mcp_add_command","cases":[{"name":"Remote HTTP server","args":["notion","http","https://mcp.notion.com/mcp"],"expected":"claude mcp add --transport http notion https://mcp.notion.com/mcp"},{"name":"User scope","args":["higgsfield","http","https://mcp.higgsfield.ai/mcp","user"],"expected":"claude mcp add --transport http --scope user higgsfield https://mcp.higgsfield.ai/mcp"},{"name":"Local server with a secret in --env","args":["airtable","stdio",["npx","-y","airtable-mcp-server"],null,{"AIRTABLE_API_KEY":"YOUR_KEY"}],"expected":"claude mcp add --env AIRTABLE_API_KEY=YOUR_KEY --transport stdio airtable -- npx -y airtable-mcp-server"},{"name":"Project scope, command with its own flags","args":["files","stdio",["python","server.py","--port","8080"],"project"],"expected":"claude mcp add --transport stdio --scope project files -- python server.py --port 8080"}]},"hints":["Start from `[\"claude\", \"mcp\", \"add\"]` and append to the list as you go: env pairs first, then `--transport`, then `--scope` if there is one.","`for key, value in (env or {}).items(): parts += [\"--env\", f\"{key}={value}\"]` handles the env pairs.","After the name: for http append the URL; for stdio append `\"--\"` and then every item of `target`."],"solution":"def mcp_add_command(name, transport, target, scope=None, env=None):\n    parts = [\"claude\", \"mcp\", \"add\"]\n    for key, value in (env or {}).items():\n        parts += [\"--env\", f\"{key}={value}\"]\n    parts += [\"--transport\", transport]\n    if scope:\n        parts += [\"--scope\", scope]\n    parts.append(name)\n    if transport == \"stdio\":\n        parts += [\"--\", *target]\n    else:\n        parts.append(target)\n    return \" \".join(parts)\n"}},{"id":"scopes-local-project-and-user","title":"Scopes: local, project and user"},{"id":"sharing-servers-in-mcp-json-without-leaking-secrets","title":"Sharing servers in .mcp.json without leaking secrets","exercise":{"prompt":"Finish `mcpJson(servers)` so it returns the object to save as a project''s shared `.mcp.json`: `{ mcpServers: { <name>: config } }`. A server with a `url` becomes `{ type: \"http\", url }`. One with a `command` becomes `{ type: \"stdio\", command, args }` (args default to `[]`), plus `env` when it has one. The file is committed, so it must never hold a secret: every env value that isn''t already a `${VAR}` reference becomes `${KEY}`, its own key name.","files":{"mcp-json.js":"function mcpJson(servers) {\n  const mcpServers = {};\n  // One entry per server, keyed by its name\n  return { mcpServers };\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { mcpJson };\n"},"check":{"type":"function","function":"mcpJson","cases":[{"name":"A remote server","args":[[{"name":"sentry","url":"https://mcp.sentry.dev/mcp"}]],"expected":{"mcpServers":{"sentry":{"type":"http","url":"https://mcp.sentry.dev/mcp"}}}},{"name":"A local server without env","args":[[{"name":"docs","command":"npx","args":["-y","docs-mcp"]}]],"expected":{"mcpServers":{"docs":{"type":"stdio","command":"npx","args":["-y","docs-mcp"]}}}},{"name":"Secrets become ${VAR} references","args":[[{"name":"db","command":"node","env":{"DB_URL":"postgres://me:hunter2@db/app","REGION":"${AWS_REGION}"}}]],"expected":{"mcpServers":{"db":{"type":"stdio","command":"node","args":[],"env":{"DB_URL":"${DB_URL}","REGION":"${AWS_REGION}"}}}}},{"name":"Several servers","args":[[{"name":"a","url":"https://a.example/mcp"},{"name":"b","command":"b-server"}]],"expected":{"mcpServers":{"a":{"type":"http","url":"https://a.example/mcp"},"b":{"type":"stdio","command":"b-server","args":[]}}}}]},"hints":["Loop with `for (const server of servers)` and set `mcpServers[server.name] = ...`.","Check `server.url` first: if it is there, the config is `{ type: \"http\", url: server.url }`.","For env, `/^\\$\\{[A-Za-z0-9_]+\\}$/.test(value)` tells you whether a value is already a reference; if it isn''t, use `\"${\" + key + \"}\"`."],"solution":"function mcpJson(servers) {\n  const mcpServers = {};\n  for (const server of servers) {\n    if (server.url) {\n      mcpServers[server.name] = { type: \"http\", url: server.url };\n      continue;\n    }\n    const config = { type: \"stdio\", command: server.command, args: server.args ?? [] };\n    if (server.env) {\n      config.env = {};\n      for (const [key, value] of Object.entries(server.env)) {\n        config.env[key] = /^\\$\\{[A-Za-z0-9_]+\\}$/.test(value) ? value : \"${\" + key + \"}\";\n      }\n    }\n    mcpServers[server.name] = config;\n  }\n  return { mcpServers };\n}\n\nmodule.exports = { mcpJson };\n"}},{"id":"signing-in-with-oauth-using-mcp","title":"Signing in with OAuth using /mcp"},{"id":"useful-servers-github-docs-databases-error-tracking","title":"Useful servers: GitHub, docs, databases, error tracking"},{"id":"mcp-safety-trusting-servers-and-prompt-injection","title":"MCP safety: trusting servers and prompt injection"}]},{"id":"images-and-video","title":"Images & Video with Claude and Higgsfield","description":"Connect Higgsfield to Claude and Claude Code, write prompts that direct a shot, and keep credits and rights under control. Optional: it needs a paid Higgsfield plan.","lessons":[{"id":"what-a-media-mcp-can-do-and-what-it-costs","title":"What a media MCP can do, and what it costs"},{"id":"connecting-higgsfield-to-claude-as-a-custom-connector","title":"Connecting Higgsfield to Claude as a custom connector"},{"id":"higgsfield-in-claude-code-the-cli-and-its-skills","title":"Higgsfield in Claude Code: the CLI and its skills"},{"id":"prompting-images-subject-style-lighting-composition","title":"Prompting images: subject, style, lighting, composition"},{"id":"prompting-video-shot-motion-camera-and-duration","title":"Prompting video: shot, motion, camera and duration"},{"id":"from-a-reference-image-to-a-video","title":"From a reference image to a video"},{"id":"budgeting-credits-before-a-batch","title":"Budgeting credits before a batch","exercise":{"prompt":"Every image or video a media MCP generates costs credits, and an agent can queue many in one go. Finish `plan_batch(costs, budget)`: run the jobs in order and stop at the first one that would take the total over `budget`. Return `{\"run\": <jobs that fit>, \"spent\": <their total>, \"left\": <budget minus spent>}`.","files":{"budget.py":"def plan_batch(costs, budget):\n    run = 0\n    spent = 0\n    # Go through the jobs in order; stop at the first that doesn''t fit\n    return {\"run\": run, \"spent\": spent, \"left\": budget - spent}\n"},"check":{"type":"function","function":"plan_batch","cases":[{"name":"Stops before going over","args":[[10,10,40,5],50],"expected":{"run":2,"spent":20,"left":30}},{"name":"Exactly on budget","args":[[5,5,5],15],"expected":{"run":3,"spent":15,"left":0}},{"name":"First job too expensive","args":[[80,1],50],"expected":{"run":0,"spent":0,"left":50}},{"name":"Nothing queued","args":[[],100],"expected":{"run":0,"spent":0,"left":100}}]},"hints":["Loop over `costs`; before adding a cost, check whether `spent + cost > budget`.","Use `break` when it doesn''t fit: later, cheaper jobs still wait, so the order of the batch is kept."],"solution":"def plan_batch(costs, budget):\n    run = 0\n    spent = 0\n    for cost in costs:\n        if spent + cost > budget:\n            break\n        spent += cost\n        run += 1\n    return {\"run\": run, \"spent\": spent, \"left\": budget - spent}\n"}},{"id":"editing-and-assembling-clips-with-ffmpeg","title":"Editing and assembling clips with ffmpeg"},{"id":"consent-copyright-and-labelling-ai-media","title":"Consent, copyright and labelling AI media"}]},{"id":"skills-commands-and-subagents","title":"Skills, Commands & Subagents","description":"Package your workflows as skills, run them with a slash, and hand side work to subagents that keep your main context clean.","lessons":[{"id":"built-in-commands-worth-knowing","title":"Built-in commands worth knowing"},{"id":"writing-a-skill-skill-md-and-its-frontmatter","title":"Writing a skill: SKILL.md and its frontmatter","exercise":{"prompt":"Finish `skill_md(name, description, instructions, manual_only=False)` so it returns the text of a `SKILL.md`: YAML frontmatter between `---` lines with `name` and `description`, plus `disable-model-invocation: true` when `manual_only` is true (so only you can run it, with `/name`), then a blank line and the instructions, ending with a newline.","files":{"skill.py":"def skill_md(name, description, instructions, manual_only=False):\n    lines = [\"---\"]\n    # Frontmatter fields, the closing ---, a blank line, then the instructions\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines)\n"},"check":{"type":"function","function":"skill_md","cases":[{"name":"A skill Claude may use on its own","args":["changelog","Writes a changelog entry from the staged diff","Read `git diff --staged` and write one entry in CHANGELOG.md."],"expected":"---\nname: changelog\ndescription: Writes a changelog entry from the staged diff\n---\n\nRead `git diff --staged` and write one entry in CHANGELOG.md.\n"},{"name":"A skill only you can run","args":["deploy","Deploys the site to production","Run the tests, then `npm run deploy`.",true],"expected":"---\nname: deploy\ndescription: Deploys the site to production\ndisable-model-invocation: true\n---\n\nRun the tests, then `npm run deploy`.\n"}]},"hints":["Add `f\"name: {name}\"` and `f\"description: {description}\"` to `lines`, and the extra line only when `manual_only` is true.","Then add `\"---\"`, an empty string for the blank line, and the instructions.","Joining with `\"\\n\"` doesn''t add a newline at the end: add `+ \"\\n\"`."],"solution":"def skill_md(name, description, instructions, manual_only=False):\n    lines = [\"---\", f\"name: {name}\", f\"description: {description}\"]\n    if manual_only:\n        lines.append(\"disable-model-invocation: true\")\n    lines += [\"---\", \"\", instructions]\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines) + \"\\n\"\n"}},{"id":"arguments-supporting-files-and-scripts-in-skills","title":"Arguments, supporting files and scripts in skills"},{"id":"when-claude-loads-a-skill-on-its-own-and-how-to-stop-it","title":"When Claude loads a skill on its own, and how to stop it"},{"id":"custom-subagents-in-claude-agents","title":"Custom subagents in .claude/agents"},{"id":"running-agents-in-parallel","title":"Running agents in parallel"},{"id":"plugins-and-marketplaces-installing-and-sharing","title":"Plugins and marketplaces: installing and sharing"},{"id":"output-styles-and-the-status-line","title":"Output styles and the status line"}]},{"id":"hooks-permissions-and-safety","title":"Hooks, Permissions & Safety","description":"Decide what Claude may do without asking, enforce rules with hooks that always run, and sandbox the rest.","lessons":[{"id":"permission-modes-from-asking-every-time-to-plan-only","title":"Permission modes, from asking every time to plan only"},{"id":"allow-and-deny-rules-in-settings-json","title":"Allow and deny rules in settings.json"},{"id":"settings-files-and-which-one-wins","title":"Settings files and which one wins"},{"id":"hooks-events-matchers-and-handlers","title":"Hooks: events, matchers and handlers"},{"id":"blocking-a-tool-call-with-a-pretooluse-hook","title":"Blocking a tool call with a PreToolUse hook","exercise":{"prompt":"A `PreToolUse` hook gets the tool call as JSON on stdin and blocks it by exiting with code 2 (code 0 lets the normal permission flow decide). Finish `decide(event)`, which gets that JSON as a dict and returns the exit code: `2` for a Bash command that starts with `rm -rf` or contains `git push --force`, and for a Read, Edit or Write of a file named `.env` or starting with `.env.`; `0` for everything else.","files":{"guard.py":"import os\n\n\ndef decide(event):\n    tool = event.get(\"tool_name\")\n    tool_input = event.get(\"tool_input\", {})\n    # Return 2 to block the tool call, 0 to let it through\n    return 0\n"},"check":{"type":"function","function":"decide","cases":[{"name":"Running tests is fine","args":[{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"npm test"}}],"expected":0},{"name":"rm -rf is blocked","args":[{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"rm -rf build"}}],"expected":2},{"name":"Force push is blocked","args":[{"tool_name":"Bash","tool_input":{"command":"git push --force origin main"}}],"expected":2},{"name":"Writing .env is blocked","args":[{"tool_name":"Write","tool_input":{"file_path":"/home/me/app/.env"}}],"expected":2},{"name":"Editing .env.local is blocked","args":[{"tool_name":"Edit","tool_input":{"file_path":"config/.env.local"}}],"expected":2},{"name":"A file that only mentions env is fine","args":[{"tool_name":"Edit","tool_input":{"file_path":"src/env.ts"}}],"expected":0},{"name":"Reading the README is fine","args":[{"tool_name":"Read","tool_input":{"file_path":"README.md"}}],"expected":0}]},"hints":["For Bash, the command is `tool_input.get(\"command\", \"\")`; use `.startswith(\"rm -rf\")` and `\"git push --force\" in command`.","For files, compare the name only: `os.path.basename(tool_input.get(\"file_path\", \"\"))`.","In a real hook you would read `json.load(sys.stdin)`, print a reason to stderr and `sys.exit(decide(event))`."],"solution":"import os\n\n\ndef decide(event):\n    tool = event.get(\"tool_name\")\n    tool_input = event.get(\"tool_input\", {})\n    if tool == \"Bash\":\n        command = tool_input.get(\"command\", \"\")\n        if command.startswith(\"rm -rf\") or \"git push --force\" in command:\n            return 2\n    if tool in (\"Read\", \"Edit\", \"Write\"):\n        name = os.path.basename(tool_input.get(\"file_path\", \"\"))\n        if name == \".env\" or name.startswith(\".env.\"):\n            return 2\n    return 0\n"}},{"id":"formatting-and-testing-after-every-edit-with-posttooluse","title":"Formatting and testing after every edit with PostToolUse"},{"id":"the-sandboxed-bash-tool-and-dev-containers","title":"The sandboxed Bash tool and dev containers"},{"id":"keeping-secrets-out-of-claude-s-reach","title":"Keeping secrets out of Claude''s reach"}]},{"id":"automation-and-the-cloud","title":"Automation: Headless, CI & the Cloud","description":"Run Claude Code from scripts and CI, let it review pull requests, and schedule work that runs while you are away.","lessons":[{"id":"headless-mode-claude-p-and-its-output-formats","title":"Headless mode: claude -p and its output formats"},{"id":"claude-code-github-actions-and-claude","title":"Claude Code GitHub Actions and @claude"},{"id":"code-review-code-review-and-pull-request-reviews","title":"Code review: /code-review and pull request reviews"},{"id":"gitlab-ci-cd","title":"GitLab CI/CD"},{"id":"claude-code-on-the-web-and-remote-control","title":"Claude Code on the web and Remote Control"},{"id":"scheduled-tasks-and-loop","title":"Scheduled tasks and /loop"},{"id":"claude-in-slack","title":"Claude in Slack"},{"id":"building-your-own-agent-with-the-agent-sdk","title":"Building your own agent with the Agent SDK"}]},{"id":"browser-and-beyond","title":"Beyond Code: Browser, Computer & Sharing","description":"Let Claude test your web app in Chrome, use the computer, publish pages as artifacts, and keep an eye on cost.","lessons":[{"id":"testing-a-web-app-with-claude-in-chrome","title":"Testing a web app with Claude in Chrome"},{"id":"reading-console-errors-and-network-requests","title":"Reading console errors and network requests"},{"id":"computer-use-from-the-cli","title":"Computer use from the CLI"},{"id":"publishing-results-as-artifacts","title":"Publishing results as artifacts"},{"id":"voice-dictation-keybindings-and-terminal-setup","title":"Voice dictation, keybindings and terminal setup"},{"id":"choosing-a-model-and-effort-level","title":"Choosing a model and effort level"},{"id":"tracking-cost-and-usage","title":"Tracking cost and usage"}]},{"id":"capstone-project","title":"Capstone Project","description":"Pick your repo option, set Claude Code up for it end to end, and ship a reviewed change.","lessons":[{"id":"planning-your-toolkit-for-the-repo-you-picked","title":"Planning your toolkit for the repo you picked"},{"id":"building-the-setup-memory-mcp-a-skill-and-a-hook","title":"Building the setup: memory, MCP, a skill and a hook"},{"id":"shipping-and-reviewing-your-change","title":"Shipping and reviewing your change"},{"id":"presenting-your-capstone","title":"Presenting your capstone"}]}]}'::jsonb)
on conflict (id) do nothing;
